  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Rounds of showers and thunderstorms with locally heavy rain,\n   the potential for debris flows, and gusty winds (mainly western\n   Montana) through today.\n\n-  Temporary relief from smoke with easterly winds.\n\n-  Gradual warming and drying trend this weekend, daily\n   thunderstorm threat continues.\n\nScattered rain showers and isolated thunderstorms continued to\nredevelop and move over western Montana and north-central Idaho\nduring the overnight hours into early this morning. Brief heavy\nrainfall and occasional lightning occurred with these storms.\nLingering showers will continue to stream over western Montana\nthrough mid-morning.\n\nThis afternoon, several rounds of showers and thunderstorms will\nimpact the Northern Rockies and will likely bring isolated\nactivity through the overnight once again. The main threat with\nthese thunderstorms will be brief heavy rainfall. Yesterday\nafternoon, thunderstorms produced up to 0.40 inches per hour,\nwhich can be expected with the thunderstorms today. The strongest\nstorms are expected between 3 PM and 9 PM, bringing a threat for\ndebris flows and excessive runoff to flood-prone areas of northern\nLemhi County and burn scars in the southern Bitterroot Valley. A\nFlash Flood Watch may be needed to highlight areas at most risk\nfor impacts this afternoon and evening. Gusty and erratic winds\nbetween 30-40 mph will also be a threat, that could blow around\nunsecured objects and bring minor tree damage.\n\nA ridge of high pressure will rebuild over the Northern Rockies\nthis weekend into early next week. Persistent southwest winds\naloft will continue to bring moisture into the region, keeping\natmospheric moisture high. This pattern will sustain daily chances\nfor afternoon showers and thunderstorms through next week,\npreventing a full return to dry conditions. Wildfire smoke will\nremain an ongoing challenge, with air quality expected to degrade\nslowly this weekend as southwest winds transport smoke plumes from\nsource fires in eastern Washington and Oregon.",
